logger -- Command

Make an entry in the system log
logger [-i] [-t tag] [-p priority] [message ...]

logger hands a message to syslog(3), which writes it into the /dev/log FIFO,
where syslogd files it according to /etc/syslog.conf.  It is the way a shell
script says something the system log should keep, and it is also the way to
find out whether the logging path is working at all: it does nothing else, so
a message that reaches /usr/adm/syslog proves every stage between.

With one or more message arguments, they are joined with single spaces and
logged as one record.  With none, the standard input is read and each line is
logged as a record of its own; empty lines are dropped.  So

    logger -t backup "level 0 started"
    make 2>&1 | logger -t make -p user.err

both work, the second filing one record per line of output.

Options
    -i		Include the process id in the tag, as `tag[pid]'.
    -t tag	Tag the records with tag instead of `logger'.
    -p pri	File the records at priority pri.  The default is
		user.notice.

A priority is `facility.level', a bare level (the facility is then user), or
a decimal priority code.  The facilities are kern, user, mail, daemon, auth,
syslog, lpr, news, uucp, cron and local0 through local7; the levels, most
urgent first, are emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info and debug.

A priority that names nothing is refused, with

    logger: unknown priority foo

and an exit status of 1, rather than being logged under some default.  A
record filed under the wrong facility is one the person looking for it will
not find.

Files
/dev/log		the FIFO syslogd reads
/etc/syslog.conf	where syslogd puts what it reads
/usr/adm/syslog		where that normally is

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it: this system has a system
log and the release the Lexicon documents has none.  Both this command and the
syslogd behind it were written for this port, and this page was written here
from their source; none of Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

Nothing is queued.  syslog(3) opens /dev/log without delay, so with no syslogd
holding the FIFO open the open fails and the record is discarded; logger still
exits 0.  A logger that prints nothing is therefore not proof that the record
was filed -- look in the file /etc/syslog.conf sends it to.

